  "live_records": {
    "q-80541": {
      "dom_id": "step-q80541",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y=2{x}^{2}+1$ and $y=3.$ If $R=[-1,1]\\times [1,3],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14157763/assets/1ca0b430b798a6dc85fd2b731a93690f79bb2c45b33bbe6f31a5618a2ce0888a.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- Functions I and II equal $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ on $D$ and equal $0$ outside $D.$\n\n- We cannot use function III. The function is not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,1)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the function is nonzero at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
