    "q-86895": {
      "dom_id": "step-q86895",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14157763/assets/f5f6dae762414eb327b9eae9e5445ef1916e9efc0e17c1316339bbeb416ec94e.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $\\{\\begin{aligned}y-x, & (x,y)\\in R \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin R\\end{aligned}$\n\nII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D \\\\ y-x, & (x,y)\\in D\\end{aligned}$\n\nIII. $\\{\\begin{aligned}y-x, & (x,y)\\in R∖D, \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R\\end{aligned}$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's draw our region $D$ and the rectangle $R:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14157763/assets/976dc1b0a7298c5cf0a331acb00594c8e4c74a14b6df3fc18f7e7997db2956c4.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal $y-x$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(y-x)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions I and III. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R\\,∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(2,0)\\in R\\,∖\\,D,$ but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function II equals $y-x$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
